"><citation citation-type="journal">Cserzo, M., Wallin, E., Simon, I., von Heijne, G. &#x00026; Elofsson, A. (1997). <italic>Protein Eng.</italic><bold>10</bold>, 673&#x02013;676.</citation></ref><ref id="bb16"><citation citation-type="journal">Dahl, S. G., Kristiansen, K. &#x00026; Sylte, I. (2002). <italic>Ann. Med.</italic><bold>34</bold>, 306&#x02013;312.</citation></ref><ref id="bb17"><citation citation-type="journal">Faulon, J. L., Sale, K. &#x00026; Young, M. (2003). <italic>Protein Sci.</italic><bold>12</bold>, 1750&#x02013;1761.</citation></ref><ref id="bb18"><citation citation-type="journal">Fenn, T. D., Ringe, D. &#x00026; Petsko, G. A. (2003). <italic>J. Appl. Cryst.</italic><bold>36</bold>, 944&#x02013;&#x000ad;947.</citation></ref><ref id="bb19"><citation citation-type="other">Hoppe, W. (1957). <italic>Acta Cryst.</italic><bold>10</bold>, 750&#x02013;751.</citation></ref><ref id="bb20"><citation citation-type="journal">Kissinger, C. R., Gehlhaar, D. K. &#x00026; Fogel, D. B. (1999). <italic>Acta Cryst.</italic> D<bold>55</bold>, 484&#x02013;491.</citation></ref><ref id="bb21"><citation citation-type="journal">Kleywegt, G. J. (1999). <italic>Acta Cryst.</italic> D<bold>55</bold>, 1878&#x02013;1884.</citation></ref><ref id="bb22"><citation citation-type="journal">Kraulis, P. J. (1991). <italic>J. Appl. Cryst.</italic><bold>24</bold>, 946&#x02013;950.</citation></ref><ref id="bb23"><citation citation-type="journal">Krogh, A., Larsson, B., von Heijne, G. &#x00026; Sonnhammer, E. L. (2001). <italic>J. Mol. Biol.</italic><bold>305</bold>, 567&#x02013;580.</citation></ref><ref id="bb24"><citation citation-type="journal">McCoy, A. J., Grosse-Kunstleve, R. W., Storoni, L. C. &#x00026; Read, R. J. (2005). <italic>Acta Cryst.</italic> D<bold>61</bold>, 458&#x02013;464.</citation></ref><ref id="bb25"><citation citation-type="journal">Perozo, E., Cortes, D. M., Sompornpisut, P., Kloda, A. &#x00026; Martinac, B. (2002). <italic>Nature (London)</italic>, <bold>418</bold>, 942&#x02013;948.</citation></ref><ref id="bb26"><citation citation-type="journal">Perozo, E., Kloda, A., Cortes, D. M. &#x00026; Martinac, B. (2001). <italic>J. Gen. Physiol.</italic><bold>118</bold>, 193&#x02013;206.</citation></ref><ref id="bb27"><citation citation-type="journal">Perrakis, A., Sixma, T. K., Wilson, K. S. &#x00026; Lamzin, V. S. (1997). <italic>Acta Cryst.</italic> D<bold>53</bold>, 448&#x02013;455.</citation></ref><ref id="bb28"><citation citation-type="journal">Raman, P., Cherezov, V. &#x00026; Caffrey, M. (2006). <italic>Cell. Mol. Life Sci.</italic><bold>63</bold>, 36&#x02013;51.</citation></ref><ref id="bb29"><citation citation-type="other">Robertson, J. M. (1935). <italic>J. Chem. Soc.</italic>, pp. 615&#x02013;621.</citation></ref><ref id="bb30"><citation citation-type="other">Rossmann, M. G. &#x00026; Blow, D. M. (1962). <italic>Acta Cryst.</italic> A<bold>15</bold>, 24&#x02013;31.</citation></ref><ref id="bb31"><citation citation-type="journal">Spencer, R. H. &#x00026; Rees, D. C. (2002). <italic>Annu. Rev. Biophys. Biomol. Struct.</italic><bold>31</bold>, 207&#x02013;233.</citation></ref><ref id="bb32"><citation citation-type="journal">Strop, P., Bass, R. &#x00026; Rees, D. C. (2003). <italic>Adv. Protein Chem.</italic><bold>63</bold>, 177&#x02013;&#x000ad;209.</citation></ref><ref id="bb33"><citation citation-type="journal">Suhre, K. &#x00026; Sanejouand, Y. H. (2004). <italic>Acta Cryst.</italic> D<bold>60</bold>, 796&#x02013;799.</citation></ref><ref id="bb34"><citation citation-type="journal">Sussman, J. L., Lin, D., Jiang, J., Manning, N. O., Prilusky, J., Ritter, O. &#x00026; Abola, E. E. (1998). <italic>Acta Cryst.</italic> D<bold>54</bold>, 1078&#x02013;1084.</citation></ref><ref id="bb35"><citation citation-type="journal">Terwilliger, T. C. (2000). <italic>Acta Cryst.</italic> D<bold>56</bold>, 965&#x02013;972.</citation></ref><ref id="bb36"><citation citation-type="journal">Tusnady, G. E., Dosztanyi, Z. &#x00026; Simon, I. (2004). <italic>Bioinformatics</italic>, <bold>20</bold>, 2964&#x02013;2972.</citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Schematic of the <italic>ab initio</italic> molecular-replacement method. Dashed lines indicate optional fine grid searches.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="d-63-00188-fig1"/></fig><fig id="fig2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Geometric description of the parameters used to generate the helical models. Constraints and limits are given in &#x000a7;<xref ref-type="sec" rid="sec2"/>2 and Table 1<xref ref-type="table" rid="table1"> &#x025b6;</xref>. Parameters of the helices in the inner bundle are subscripted h1, while the parameters of the outer helical bundle are subscripted h2. (<italic>a</italic>) The first helix is rotated with Euler angles &#x003b1;<sub>h1</sub>, &#x003b2;<sub>h1</sub> and &#x003b3;<sub>h1</sub>. The resulting new helix orientation is shown in a lighter shade of gray. (<italic>b</italic>) The second helix is rotated with Euler angles &#x003b1;<sub>h2</sub>, &#x003b2;<sub>h2</sub> and &#x003b3;<sub>h2</sub> and a distance from the protein symmetry axis <italic>r</italic>                  <sub>h2</sub>. (<italic>c</italic>) &#x003b1;<sub>b</sub> is the rotation of the outer helical bundle about the inner helical bundle.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="d-63-00188-fig2"/></fig><fig id="fig3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Molecular replacement results. (<italic>a</italic>), (<italic>c</italic>) <italic>Z</italic>                   <italic>versus</italic> LLG scatter plot from coarse molecular replacement searches of inner (<italic>a</italic>) and outer (<italic>c</italic>) helical bundle ensembles. Each black square represents the top solution of one model. (<italic>b</italic>), (<italic>d</italic>) NCS*OMIT product scores resulting from coarse search for inner (<italic>b</italic>) and outer (<italic>d</italic>) helical bundle ensembles. In all panels, the top ten <italic>Z</italic>, LLG and <italic>Z</italic>*LLG scores are shown in red, the best solution from a coarse search is shown in blue, and the best solution from a fine grid search is shown in green. For clarity, the best coarse and fine search solutions are also shown as large squares in panels (<italic>a</italic>) and (<italic>c</italic>).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="d-63-00188-fig3"/></fig><fig id="fig4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Comparison of (<italic>a</italic>) structure and (<italic>b</italic>) <italic>R</italic>                  <sub>free</sub> statistic for the top five converging (light blue, dark blue and green) and nonconverging (black and gray) models after torsion-angle simulated-annealing refinement with a maximum-likelihood target function. The known transmembrane structure of MscL (red) is shown for reference.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="d-63-00188-fig4"/></fig><fig id="fig5" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>(<italic>a</italic>) Comparison of the known transmembrane helical structure of MscL (red) and the model derived from <italic>ab initio</italic> molecular replacement (blue). (<italic>b</italic>) View perpendicular to the membrane normal. (<italic>c</italic>) Ribbon representation of the known MscL structure. (<italic>d</italic>) Ribbon representation of the transmembrane and cytoplasmic helices built by <italic>ARP</italic>/<italic>wARP HelixBuild</italic> into electron density computed with phases from <italic>ab initio</italic> molecular replacement. This figure was prepared with <italic>PyMOL</italic> (DeLano Scientific, San Carlos, CA, USA) and <italic>POVSCRIPT</italic> (Fenn <italic>et al.</italic>, 2003<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bb18"> &#x025b6;</xref>; Kraulis, 1991<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bb22"> &#x025b6;</xref>)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="d-63-00188-fig5"/></fig><fig id="fig6" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>(<italic>a</italic>) Anomalous difference electron-density map (red) contoured at 4&#x003c3; computed for a gold derivative using the phases obtained from <italic>ab initio</italic> molecular replacement. 2<italic>F</italic>                  <sub>o</sub> &#x02212; <italic>F</italic>                  <sub>c</sub> map contoured at 1&#x003c3; computed from <italic>ab initio</italic> phases with omitted residues shown in green for (<italic>b</italic>) the complete transmembrane ensemble, (<italic>c</italic>) for a single transmembrane helix and (<italic>d</italic>) for the cytoplasmic domain (for clarity, the electron density is shown for only one cytoplasmic helix).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="d-63-00188-fig6"/></fig><fig id="fig7" position="float"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Determination of the oligomeric state from X-ray diffraction data by <italic>ab initio</italic> molecular replacement. (<italic>a</italic>) <italic>Z versus</italic> LLG scatter plot from molecular replacement for models with fourfold (red), fivefold (green) and sixfold (blue) symmetry. (<italic>b</italic>) NCS and OMIT scores for fourfold, fivefold and sixfold assemblies. (<italic>c</italic>) Refinement progress monitored with <italic>R</italic>                  <sub>free</sub> statistics of the top five models from each symmetry group. Colours are as in (<italic>a</italic>).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="d-63-00188-fig7"/></fig><table-wrap id="table1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><title>Parameters used for model building according to the geometry indicated in Fig. 2<xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig2"> &#x025b6;</xref>               </title><p>The increment of the &#x003b1;<sub>h<italic>i</italic></sub> angle is dependent on the tilting angle &#x003b2;<sub>h<italic>i</italic></sub> and is calculated by dividing the circumference described by the end of the tilted helix by the helical spacing <italic>s</italic> as described in the methods<xref ref-type="sec" ri